{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-152259","model_call":21,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gothic rock\" (+ bleak gloomy dark introspective romantic) (+ lyrics genre))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gothic rock\" (+ bleak gloomy dark introspective romantic) (+ lyrics genre))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1236837256#2_1363402518","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2353","rank":1,"snippet":": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1236837256#2_1363402518\" , \"end_char\": 3215.000000 , \"headings\": \"Gothic rock Gothic rock From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Contents Characteristics History Forerunners (1960s and 1970s) Origins Expansion of the scene Subsequent developments Visual elements Impact See also References Bibliography Books Journals External links \" , \"segment\": \"Reynolds described the vocal style as consisting of \\\"deep, droning alloys of Jim Morrison and Leonard Cohen \\\". Several acts used drum machines downplaying the rhythm's backbeat. <cover>Gothic rock typically deals with dark themes addressed through lyrics </cover>and the music's atmosphere. The poetic sensibilities of the genre led gothic rock lyrics to exhibit literary romanticism, morbidity, existentialism, religious symbolism or supernatural mysticism. History Forerunners (1960s and 1970s) The Doors performing live in 1968. They have been cited as a major influence in the gothic rock genre. Critic John Stickney used the term \"gothic rock\" to describe the music of the Doors in October 1967, in a review published in The Williams Record. Stickney wrote that the band met the journalists \"in the gloomy vaulted wine cellar of the Delmonico hotel, the ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1236837256#2_1363402518","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":479},"model_calls":21,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
