{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-152259","model_call":21,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2353"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2353"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3483373456#0_3851345232","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2353","rank":4,"snippet":"ethereal wave gothic rock neoclassical neofolk Neue Deutsche Todeskunst Regional scenes France Germany Dark wave, or darkwave, is a music genre that emerged from the new wave and post-punk movement of the late 1970s. Dark wave compositions are largely based on minor key tonality and introspective lyrics and have been perceived as being dark, romantic and bleak, with an undertone of sorrow. Common features include the use of chordophones such as electric and acoustic guitar, violin and piano, as well as electronic instruments such as synthesizer, sampler and drum machine. The <cover>genre embraces a range of styles including cold wave, ethereal wave, gothic rock, neoclassical dark </cover>wave and neofolk. In the 1980s, a subculture developed primarily in Europe alongside dark wave music, whose followers were called wavers or dark wavers. In some countries such as Germany, the movement also included fans of gothic rock (so-called trad-goths ). Contents 1 History 1.1 Origins in Europe 1.1.1 1980s: Foundations 1.1.2 1990s: The 2nd generation 1.2 United States 2 See also 3 References 4 Bibliography 5 External links History Origins in Europe 1980s:\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 ,","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1236837256#2_1363402518","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1236837256#0_1363397743","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1236837256#1_1363400322","rank":3,"relevance":3}]},"time":7},"model_calls":21,"document_judgments":17,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
