{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-152259","model_call":36,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2367"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2367"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_778013205#2_1570772496","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2367","rank":3,"snippet":"Young Blood” Eels, “Fresh Blood” Bauhaus, “Bela Lugosi’s Dead” Misfits, “Wolfs Blood” Sopor Æternus & the Ensemble of Shadows, “Children of the Corn” Celtic Frost, “Danse Macabre” Black S abbath, “Black Sabbath” Throbbing Gristle, Devil’s Gateway Ruth White, “Flowers of Evil: Litanies of Satan” \" , \"segment\": \"Bauhaus, “Bela Lugosi’s Dead” It takes nearly three minutes of guitar squalls, minimalist drum tapping and a hypnotic bass line before Bauhaus frontman Peter Murphy's menacing <cover>lyrics about Count Dracula (and the titular actor who played him in the 1931 cinema classic) make their unsettling introduction. The group’s first single, released in 1979, remains a goth-rock milestone thanks to its bare-bones darkness. </cover>Misfits, “Wolfs Blood” Glenn Danzig might not seem as intimidating today, what with the photos of him out and about buying cat food, but as the muscular leader of the Misfits, flanked by bassist Jerry Only and his brother, guitarist Doyle Wolfgang von Frankenstein, he was one of the first punk stars with an image based on horror and sci-fi. Here, Danzig screams like a raving asylum patient about turning into a werewolf -- and depending on what lyrics sites you ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1346695066#6_1842370242","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_1595724340#2_3301623748","rank":2,"relevance":1}]},"time":8},"model_calls":36,"document_judgments":27,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
