{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-152259","model_call":47,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gothic rock\" (+ \"genre overview\" \"music genre\" allmusic) (+ gloomy somber romantic lyrics))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gothic rock\" (+ \"genre overview\" \"music genre\" allmusic) (+ gloomy somber romantic lyrics))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3483373456#0_3851345232","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2377","rank":1,"snippet":"Dark wave - Wikipedia Dark wave From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to navigation Jump to search Genre of music For the 1956 documentary film, see The Dark Wave. Dark wave Other names Doom doom wave Stylistic origins New wave post-punk Cultural origins Early 1980s, Europe (particularly in the United Kingdom, West Germany, France and Italy) Typical instruments Guitar bass guitar drums drum machine synthesizer sampler violin cello piano percussion Subgenres Cabaret noir cold wave ethereal wave<cover>gothic rock neoclassical neofolk Neue Deutsche Todeskunst Regional scenes France Germany Dark wave, or darkwave, is a music genre that emerged from the new wave and post-punk movement of the late 1970s. Dark wave compositions are largely based on minor key tonality and introspective lyrics </cover>and have been perceived as being dark, romantic and bleak, with an undertone of sorrow. Common features include the use of chordophones such as electric and acoustic guitar, violin and piano, as well as electronic instruments such as synthesizer, sampler and drum machine. The genre embraces a range of styles including cold wave, ethereal wave, gothic rock, neoclassical dark wave and neofolk. In the 1980s, a subculture developed primarily in Europe alongside dark wave music, whose followers ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_3483373456#0_3851345232","rank":1,"relevance":1}]},"time":304},"model_calls":47,"document_judgments":34,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
