{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-152259","model_call":59,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2388"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2388"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_970349970#29_2058892363","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2388","rank":4,"snippet":"Metal Bands of the ‘90s #81—100 Gothic Metal in the ‘90s Heavy metal fashion revolves around dark colors. Other Notable Heavy Metal Bands of the ‘90s Groove Metal in the ‘90s \" , \"segment\": \"Dark Tranquility 97. Corrosion of Conformity 98. Beherit 99. Entombed 100. Tristania Gothic Metal in the ‘90s Gothic metal, also referred to as goth metal, gained mainstream attention in the ‘90s. This style of music combined traditional heavy metal with dark atmospheric <cover>gothic rock influences. This form of music evolved out of the death-doom movement, which was a fusion of death metal and doom metal. This genre stands out for its use of confessional and introspective lyrics, often </cover>centered around gothic fiction. This blending of heavy metal and goth rock can still be heard in the compositions of more symphonic metal bands. Certain gothic metal acts relied on a \"beauty and the beast\" singing style, in which the male vocalist showcases an aggressive singing style while the female vocalist exhibits a clean singing style that's meant as a point of contrast.\" , \"start_char\": 8123.000000 , \"title\": \"100 ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_1437813071#0_3049767224","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_1437813071#1_3049772449","rank":2,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3261615973#0_3374559745","rank":3,"relevance":1}]},"time":6},"model_calls":59,"document_judgments":43,"relevance_3":3,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
